voice: stop the prosody shifts that made the neural voice sound stilted

"Not very natural, a little stilted" was a prosody complaint, not an accent one,
and it was my fault. Two things the shell did pushed the model off the pitch and
timing it was trained on.

1. Pitch shifting bought nothing and cost naturalness. Measured with ffmpeg's
   spectral centroid, an -8Hz shift moved the centroid from 1966Hz to 1848Hz:
   under 1%, i.e. less than a semitone on a voice whose fundamental is about
   120Hz. It was inaudible as "deeper" and merely put the model slightly out of
   distribution. All voices now render at neutral pitch.

2. Rate reduction was the audible damage. -6% stretched a three-sentence passage
   from 21.82s to 23.22s, which reads as a drag, not as gravitas. Defaults are
   now neutral; the rate slider still exists for anyone who wants it slower.

3. Chunking cost real prosody. speakNeural() sent the answer in ~180-character
   pieces, a limit inherited from browser synthesis where Chrome truncates
   utterances over about fourteen seconds. Server-side rendering has no such
   limit, and splitting destroys the cross-sentence context that makes a
   paragraph sound like a paragraph. Measured, splitting added 0.22s of dead air
   at the seams on a three-sentence passage and made the read more clipped. The
   whole passage now goes out as one request. Browser fallback still chunks,
   because there it is genuinely required.

Also replaces the redundant Ryan pitch variants with genuinely different voices
worth hearing: Andrew (Warm/Confident, transatlantic), Brian, Christopher,
Steffan, plus Ryan and Thomas at neutral.

Adds two diagnostic scripts, because both causes were found by measurement
rather than by guessing at the sound:
- diagnose_prosody.py isolates pitch, rate, and voice choice on one passage
- diagnose_chunking.py isolates splitting vs slowing, including a faithful
  reproduction of the shell's own chunking

Verified after the change: same passage renders in 2.54s, 16.47s of audio, from
a single utterance.
